new_candidates.body:
`Tree::~Tree()` calls `haltTree()` (`src/bt_factory.cpp:719`). A destructor is implicitly `noexcept`, so if any node throws while halting, the process aborts in `std::terminate`.

One easy way to get there is `TryCatch catch_on_halt="true"`: `TryCatchNode::halt()` ticks the catch child, and a tick is allowed to throw. The tree-exec fuzzer in #1277 found this; it currently avoids the case by never generating `catch_on_halt="true"`.

### Reproducer (master, 1a21a784)

```cpp
class Thrower : public BT::SyncActionNode {
public:
  Thrower(const std::string& n, const BT::NodeConfig& c) : SyncActionNode(n, c) {}
  static BT::PortsList providedPorts() { return {}; }
  BT::NodeStatus tick() override { throw std::runtime_error("cleanup failed"); }
};

// <TryCatch catch_on_halt="true"> <Sleep msec="1000"/> <Thrower/> </TryCatch>
auto tree = factory.createTreeFromText(xml);
tree.tickExactlyOnce();   // RUNNING
// tree goes out of scope
```

```
terminate called after throwing an instance of 'BT::NodeExecutionError'
  what():  Exception in node 'Thrower::3' [Thrower]: cleanup failed
Aborted (core dumped)
```

On master, catching doesn't help either. If the user calls `tree.haltTree()` inside a `try`/`catch` before the tree is destroyed, that first exception is caught, but `~Tree()` still aborts. `TryCatchNode::halt()` throws before it reaches `ControlNode::halt()`, so the node stays RUNNING, and the destructor halts it a second time and throws again.

### Relation to #1273
#1273 (reworked) adds a guard to `TreeNode::haltNode()`: when `halt()` throws, the node's status is reset to IDLE before the exception propagates. That fixes the second scenario (catch `haltTree()`, then destroy the tree). The first scenario still terminates, because there the first throw happens inside `~Tree()` itself.

### Possible fix
- Don't let exceptions escape `~Tree()`: catch around `haltTree()` and report them on stderr, as other destructors do.
- `TryCatchNode::halt()` still skips resetting `in_catch_`/`current_child_idx_` when the catch child throws. A try/catch + rethrow there would leave the node fully reset.
- Once fixed, #1277's fuzzer can generate `catch_on_halt="true"` again.
